I love the book of Ruth. I really do.

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
It's a simple story with a great many huge implications.

6
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Ryan started it last week. We're in a small short series on it.

7
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Today we're going to cover the rest of the first chapter,

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
but we're going to read the whole first chapter together, okay?

9
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
You don't have to read it out loud together. Just read along. I'll read it out loud for us.

10
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
So here we go.

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
"In the days when the judges ruled, there was a famine in the land.

12
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
And a man of Bethlehem and Judah went to sojourn in the country of Moab,

13
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
and he and his wife and his two sons.

14
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The name of the man was Elimelech, the name of his wife Naomi,

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
and the names of their two sons were Malan and Chilion.

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
They were Ephrathites from Bethlehem and Judah.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
They went to the country of Moab, and they remained there.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
But Elimelech, the husband of Naomi, died, and she was left with her two sons.

19
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,000
These took Moabite wives.

20
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
The name of one was Orpah, the other was Ruth.

21
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
And they lived there about ten years.

22
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
And then both Malan and Chilion died.

23
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
So the woman was left without her two sons and her husband.

24
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Then she arose with her daughters-in-law to return from the land of Moab,

25
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
because she'd heard that in the land of Moab that the Lord had visited his people

26
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:05,000
by giving them food.

27
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
So she departed from the place where she was and her two daughters-in-law with her,

28
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
and they went on the way to return to the land of Judah.

29
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
But Naomi said to her daughters-in-law, "Go, return each of you to your mother's house.

30
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
May the Lord deal kindly with you as you've dealt with the dead and with me.

31
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
May the Lord grant you that you may find a place of rest, each of you in the house of her husband."

32
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,000
And then she kissed him.

33
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
And they raised their voices, and they wept.

34
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
However, they said to her, "No, we will return with you to your people."

35
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
But Naomi said, "Return, my daughters. Why should you go with me?

36
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Do I still have sons in my womb, that they would be your husbands?

37
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Return, my daughters. For I'm too old to have a husband.

38
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
If I said I had hope, if I were even to have a husband tonight and give birth to sons,

39
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
would you therefore wait until they were grown?

40
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Would you therefore refrain from marrying? No, my daughters.

41
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
It's much more bitter for me than for you,

42
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
because the hand of the Lord has come out against me."

43
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
And they raised up their voices, and they wept again.

44
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
And Orpah kissed her mother-in-law, but Ruth clung to her.

45
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And then she said, "Behold, your sister-in-law has gone back to her people,

46
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
and her gods. Return after your sister-in-law."

47
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
But Ruth said, "Do not plead with me to leave you or turn back from following you.

48
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Where you go, I'll go. Where you sleep, I will sleep.

49
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Your people shall be my people, and your God, my God.

50
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Where you die, there will I die, and there will I be buried.

51
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
May the Lord do so to me, and worse, if anything but death separates me from you."

52
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
And when she saw that she was determined to go with her, she stopped speaking to her about it.

53
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
So they went on until they came to Bethlehem.

54
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
And when they'd come to Bethlehem, all the city was stirred because of them.

55
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
And the women said, "Is this Naomi?"

56
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
But she said to them, "Do not call me Naomi. Call me Mara.

57
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
For the Almighty has dealt very bitterly with me.

58
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
I went away full, and the Lord has brought me back empty.

59
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Why do you call me Naomi, since the Lord has testified against me, and the Almighty has afflicted me?"

60
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
And so it was, Naomi returned with her, Ruth the Moabitess, her daughter-in-law, who returned from the land of Moab.

61
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
And they came to Bethlehem at the beginning of the barley harvest."

62
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,000
All right.

63
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
So this is a simple story.

64
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
And it takes place at the time of the barley harvest.

65
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,000
When's that?

66
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,000
It's right now.

67
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Barley is a winter grain, so you would harvest it right at the beginning of spring.

68
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:24,000
You would begin harvesting it.

69
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
So it's right at this part of the calendar year that the story that we just read is taking place.

70
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
It's when things look dead.

71
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It's the story of a harvest after a hard winter.

72
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
When things look dead, but they're beginning to grow and blossom in the spring.

73
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
It's a story of life growing out of the ashes of suffering.

74
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
But today, we're just going to talk about the ashes of suffering.

75
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Okay?

76
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Because that's what this chapter is focused on.

77
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
We're going to look at the suffering.

78
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,000
So suffering.

79
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
I keep toying with the idea.

80
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
I should step out from behind the pulpit when I say this, right?

81
00:04:58,000 --> 00:04:59,000
It's not gospel.

82
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:10,000
But I keep toying with the idea that in Scripture, all the books of poetry sort of function to emotionally connect with us during different times in our life as we go through suffering in different ways.

83
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Proverbs in our youth when we're learning the rules.

84
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Ecclesiastes for the younger adults in us that's asking what does it all mean.

85
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Existential suffering there.

86
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
The song of Solomon for when we suffer in love.

87
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Psalms is for that rough middle of our life when we're tossed about and we don't really know how to pray.

88
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Job for the insuperable loss and despair as we sometimes encounter.

89
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Lamentations for the incomprehensible tragedies that others built to visit upon us, right?

90
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
And we often think about Job as the book of suffering.

91
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,000
But each one of these deals with suffering in a way appropriate to different phases of our life.

92
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
And then we have Ruth, right?

93
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Where we see suffering serving the function of helping us move back his direction.

94
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
And there's two options in which way we go.

95
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Suffering is one of the main things that drives people to God, looking for ultimate answers.

96
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
It's also one of the main things that drives people away from God.

97
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
They start blaming and questioning.

98
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Where's God?

99
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:16,000
And we see Naomi and Ruth kind of going different directions as they go through the same suffering together.

100
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
In chapter one.

101
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
To help us, I don't want to say this to compare.

102
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
They had it rough.

103
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,000
But I want to make sure that we can empathize with them and really understand what they're going through.

104
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Because that's going to be important as we go through the rest of the book of Ruth, okay?

105
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
So, there's many types of physical suffering.

106
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,000
Actually, if you do any research, even if you just Google and look at AI's results,

107
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
you will see an enormous number of ways of cataloging suffering.

108
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Mankind spends a lot of time trying to explain to ourselves how we're suffering and why we're suffering and what suffering means.

109
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Because it's suffering.

110
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:53,000
We don't like it.

111
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
There's physical suffering, aging, pain, injury, illness.

112
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:04,000
There's psychological suffering, grief, loss, anxiety, fear, depression, guilt, shame.

113
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:10,000
Social suffering, isolation, injustice, conflict.

114
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
There's existential suffering.

115
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Struggling with the meaning and purpose of what's going on around you, even in your suffering.

116
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
And suffering that begets more suffering.

117
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,000
From personal experience, I know that when you go through a suffering time, people pull back from you.

118
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
They don't want to be infected by it, right?

119
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
You have trouble.

120
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,000
You suffer.

121
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,000
People step away.

122
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,000
You feel isolated.

123
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Maybe that's when you step into a pattern of addiction.

124
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
Make more bad choices.

125
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,000
Suffer more.

126
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,000
People step further away.

127
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,000
It's this cycle.

128
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:45,000
There's unfulfilled expectation, which is common in the West.

129
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,000
That's the West modern tragedy.

130
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:49,000
And it feels like that's nothing compared to real tragedy.

131
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:50,000
But we are suffering from it.

132
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Don't undermine it, right?

133
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Unfulfilled expectations.

134
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
That was the seventh betrayal.

135
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
That's the bottom level of hell, according to Dante.

136
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
That's as bad as it gets.

137
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
There's self-inflicted suffering.

138
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,000
The Greek tragedy.

139
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
There's the fallen world around us.

140
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
That's the tragedy in the Bible.

141
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Many people call it acts of God.

142
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:16,000
They see it as divine discipline and suffering that refines us.

143
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
And that's clearly what Naomi's thinking here.

144
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:26,000
She thinks that she has been unfaithful, gone to Moab, and all this suffering has been visited upon her by a God who's disciplining her.

145
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,000
Right?

146
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:28,000
And she's bitter about it.

147
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:37,000
But the important thing to recognize is that all those things I just read that you go through, Naomi has experienced all of them.

148
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
All of them.

149
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
In the first five verses of this chapter.

150
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
Don't let it flow past you quickly just because it's quickly read.

151
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Now, again, not for comparison, not to say she had it worse than us.

152
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
But you should be able to empathize with some of these things as we go through them.

153
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
In verse one, she's moving.

154
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Right?

155
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
I know that some of you are moving right now.

156
00:08:58,000 --> 00:08:59,000
It's really stressful.

157
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
You guys, you're telling me how stressful it is to move.

158
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:02,000
Right?

159
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And she's, apart from anything else going on in her life, she had to move to a foreign country.

160
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,000
And at the end, she's moving back, dislocating her nest, as it were.

161
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:17,000
In psychology today, moving was listed as the number five most stressful thing behind, like, death and divorce.

162
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Right?

163
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
So she's moving.

164
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
But this is more like moving out of a war zone.

165
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,000
Right?

166
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:26,000
She's moving because there's no food and her home has been torn up.

167
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
So it's an extra stressful moment.

168
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,000
In verse one, she's moving to an unfriendly foreign nation.

169
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:38,000
And to appreciate what that means, imagine that you have to leave Winchester because there's no food.

170
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:42,000
And the place you can find to go with food is, let's say, Iran.

171
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,000
It's a place where your ethnicity, your nationality and your religion are not welcome.

172
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,000
No one wants you there.

173
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,000
No one likes you there.

174
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:49,000
And you don't know anybody there.

175
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
There's no way to get on the grid there.

176
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Right?

177
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
So that's what they've done.

178
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
They're doing that because of the political chaos.

179
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
The chapter starts with in the time of the judges.

180
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,000
The time of judges was a time when, if there was 40 years with no invasion and slaughter,

181
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
it was mentioned as a historical high point.

182
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Right?

183
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Every eight to 10 years, a new army would come through and ravage the countryside.

184
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
And take the food and run the people into caves.

185
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
And very likely this is where the famine came from.

186
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:22,000
Right?

187
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,000
We're told that these are people of Bethlehem.

188
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,000
Bethlehem, house of bread.

189
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:31,000
This was known from ancient times as a very fertile place with fertile soil.

190
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
So it was a target for invasions.

191
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:34,000
Right?

192
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,000
It's a place where they've moved because it seems like a great place to live.

193
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
But it's turned into chaos and they have to move to a foreign land where they're not welcome.

194
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
This is an incredible amount of suffering and we're still in verse one.

195
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Okay?

196
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
Um, they have hunger.

197
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,000
They're in this place where they're not accepted and they have two sons.

198
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
Now, the language is a little ambiguous.

199
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
My best guess, and I could be wrong.

200
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:06,000
My best educated guess is that their sons were in the 12 to 14 range when they moved to Moab.

201
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
They were trying to move a place where their sons could have opportunity.

202
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Right?

203
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:14,000
And that would mean 10 years later they would have been in the 22, 24 range.

204
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:18,000
Young adults when they would, when Naomi moved back.

205
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Newly married.

206
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
So their newly married brides are still with Naomi.

207
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:25,000
So that's the time when you need a father.

208
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:30,000
These young men need to be set out to apprenticeship, to trade.

209
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
They need to learn how to deal with their hormones.

210
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
They need to know how to set up a household.

211
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,000
They need to know how to integrate with culture and be a man in the head of the house.

212
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Naomi doesn't have a man.

213
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Her husband died.

214
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
She doesn't have any uncles.

215
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,000
She doesn't have any grandfathers.

216
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:48,000
She's on her own in a foreign country where the customs are foreign.

217
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And she's having to lead these young men through their teen years on her own.

218
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
That's a kind of suffering.

219
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
I've suffered staying here with people I know and having a couple of teenagers.

220
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,000
Right?

221
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:07,000
I can't even imagine doing it, you know, in a war torn zone and having to move to Iran, for lack of another comparative.

222
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Her husband died.

223
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
And she had to direct her two sons into careers and marriages.

224
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
And they had to have weddings.

225
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Weddings on that list of the things that are really stressful.

226
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
Just a good wedding.

227
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Really stressful.

228
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,000
She had two weddings where the children chose women from a different faith and religion and stepped away from her.

229
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
This happens all the time with immigrant families.

230
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Right?

231
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:34,000
You bring your child to a country for great opportunity and what you get is a loss of children who go into the culture and leave you.

232
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Right?

233
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
And that's what she's experienced.

234
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:41,000
Her husbands took, I don't, it's a terrible choice.

235
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:50,000
They have to set up a household because, I don't know if you recognize this, but at the time, legal structures, you interacted with the law through the father's house that you lived in.

236
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:55,000
And you integrated with social structures through the father's household, the reputation of the father's household.

237
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,000
They don't have a father.

