00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:02,000
He wrote, "The meaning of earthly existence lies not as we've grown used to thinking and prospering,

461
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:06,000
but in the development of our souls."

462
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:10,000
That's what they came to after that giant failed experiment.

463
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:15,000
You can't put prospering as your idol.

464
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:21,000
As C.S. Lewis says, "If we find within ourselves a desire that nothing in this world can satisfy,

465
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:24,000
the most probable explanation is that we're made for another world.

466
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:27,000
Probably, earthly pleasures were never meant to satisfy,

467
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:31,000
but only to arouse, to suggest the real thing."

468
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:37,000
All that we call human history, money, poverty, ambition, war, prostitution, classes, empire, slavery,

469
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:45,000
all that Naomi's going through is the long terrible story of man trying to find something other than God which can make him happy.

470
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:50,000
So we have two women going through this kind of suffering.

471
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
And they're going different directions.

472
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:56,000
One had the option to pursue righteous, but she did what was right in her own eyes.

473
00:28:56,000 --> 00:29:00,000
Now they have the option to either blame God or claim God as their own.

474
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:11,000
And the role that we see God playing in our suffering will dramatically affect how we experience hardship.

475
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:16,000
And Ruth is inadvertently going to lead Naomi into a better way of experiencing hardship.

476
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:19,000
Just through simple faithfulness.

477
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:26,000
So let's walk through the new passage in Ruth 1:6-22.

478
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:33,000
Ruth and Naomi, it says that they have, in verse 6,

479
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,000
"She arose with their daughters-in-law and returned from the land of Moab

480
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:40,000
because she'd heard in the land of Moab that the Lord had visited his people by giving them food."

481
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:41,000
Right?

482
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:43,000
"There's food, safe to go back."

483
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:47,000
"It's a three to four day walk from what's modern Joban Jordan to Bethlehem."

484
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:54,000
So, two paupered, impoverished women on their own walking for four days.

485
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:56,000
That's suffering by itself.

486
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:57,000
Right?

487
00:29:57,000 --> 00:29:58,000
And that's what they're doing.

488
00:29:58,000 --> 00:29:59,000
They're walking back.

489
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:02,000
And I imagine in my mind when they get to the border between the countries,

490
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:06,000
there's a little rise and Naomi stops and says to her daughters-in-law,

491
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:07,000
"Okay, time to go back."

492
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:08,000
Right?

493
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:09,000
"I'm doing this on my own.

494
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,000
We are no longer one but two groups of people."

495
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,000
Right?

496
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:17,000
And in this passage, there are four characters, but there's really three.

497
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:22,000
Orpah is just a passing character that stands in contrast to Ruth, okay?

498
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:29,000
The names that are mentioned frequently are Naomi, Ruth, and the Lord.

499
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:33,000
These are the three characters that the author is showing us how they relate to each other.

500
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:39,000
And if you don't know this, parenthetical, in your Bible when it says Lord, L-O-R-D, and it's all normal cases,

501
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:44,000
that just means someone who rules a piece of land, the local king or ruler, potentate.

502
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
He's the Lord of the area.

503
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:51,000
But if it's Lord, L, capital O-R-D, all uppercase, drop case,

504
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:58,000
that's the author, the translator's effort to carry on the tradition of showing reverence by not writing the name of God.

505
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:03,000
In the Old Testament, they would use four letters that sound like Yahweh when you say it out loud.

506
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:07,000
We say Yahweh, breath of God, Yahweh.

507
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:09,000
And they would use that as a placeholder.

508
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:12,000
And everybody knew that meant God, but they didn't want to say his name out of reverence.

509
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:17,000
In the New Testament, I'm sorry, in our Bibles today, it says L-O-R-D, all uppercase.

510
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:20,000
And that means name of God, okay?

511
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:25,000
So the name of God is referenced seven times and nine times, they call him the Almighty twice.

512
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:28,000
The most referenced person in this chapter, okay?

513
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:33,000
So they're talking about how Naomi and Ruth relate to Yahweh.

514
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:45,000
In 613, this whole section, it's just a litany of bitterness that Naomi's saying how God is judging her and has made her life bitter.

515
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:55,000
I think she recognizes, rightly or wrongly, that this is being visited on her because of her unfaithfulness, but she's not bending her neck to it.

516
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:56,000
She just feels like she's been ruined.

517
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:58,000
Her life is over, right?

518
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:05,000
She says in the end, don't call me Naomi, which means sweet, call me pleasant, call me Mara, which means bitter.

519
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:10,000
There's nothing less but this husk of bitterness in verse 20.

520
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:13,000
And she feels judged, and she feels unloved.

521
00:32:13,000 --> 00:32:21,000
She's had everything we've talked about this morning, all that suffering on her shoulders, and she believes she brought it on herself.

522
00:32:21,000 --> 00:32:28,000
And God is judging her because of all the stuff she did, and there's nothing she can do about it in her life as an ash heap.

523
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:31,000
And that's where Naomi is.

524
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:35,000
So, she tells her daughters to leave her three times.

525
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:38,000
The first time, in verse 8 and 9, she says, "I'm not your mother."

526
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:50,000
We talked about how important it was to have a father's house at the time, but it was also true that a widowed daughter would go back to the apartments of her mother in their home.

527
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:59,000
And her mother would help her through the grieving process, help her get rehabilitated, reintroduce her to the community, help her get ready to go out there, find another man, build another house.

528
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:00,000
That's the way it worked.

529
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:05,000
So, it was important that your mother take care of you and rehabilitate you in the house of your father.

530
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:09,000
And Naomi, instead of going back to the house of your father, she calls out their mother, right?

531
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:11,000
Go back to your mother's house.

532
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:12,000
I'm not your mother.

533
00:33:12,000 --> 00:33:14,000
I can't do that stuff for you, right?

534
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:16,000
That's what she's saying.

535
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:20,000
And they respond to that.

536
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:24,000
She says, "And may Yahweh give you what he didn't give me," right?

537
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:27,000
In verse 9, "May he give you kindness and rest and husbands."

538
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:30,000
If you come with me, I can't give you those things.

539
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:32,000
He might give it to you.

540
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:34,000
They respond to her with the language of otherness.

541
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:39,000
"Oh no, let we want to come with you and your people," right?

542
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:41,000
So, this divide is there.

543
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:45,000
Verse 2 then, after they've cried and said, "No, we don't want to leave you."

544
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:51,000
She makes it more clear, "Not do you need to go back to your mother, but there is no future if you stay with me."

545
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:56,000
Explicit description of how they will only suffer if they come with her, she says.

546
00:33:56,000 --> 00:34:01,000
In menopause, in verse 11, her life and purpose, they've just passed her by.

547
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:09,000
I spoke at a church recently which was struggling and they, I said, essentially, you know, "Well, what's your vision for the future?"

548
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:11,000
And they said, "We had our vision, it didn't work," right?

549
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:13,000
And they're just depressed.

550
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:17,000
This church went through that but came out the other side.

551
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:19,000
We've seen the regrowth.

552
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:22,000
It's difficult to get past your suffering and on to growth.

553
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:23,000
How do you do that?

554
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:28,000
You change your direction and hopefully you have somebody like Ruth in your life, right?

555
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:35,000
In verse 13, she says, "Don't kid yourself.

556
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:40,000
The feelings of affection you have for me aren't going to last," right?

557
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:46,000
"Are you really going to go all that time without having another husband just because you like me?"

558
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:49,000
"Are you really going to stay outside of culture and unattached?"

559
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:51,000
"No, you're not going to do that.

560
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:52,000
You're going to want to have sons.

561
00:34:52,000 --> 00:34:53,000
Don't kid yourself."

562
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:58,000
And in verse 13, she says explicitly, "Yahweh is against me.

563
00:34:58,000 --> 00:35:01,000
If you come with me, he's going to be against you too," is the implication, right?

564
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:03,000
"You don't want to be with me."

565
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:10,000
At which point, in verse 9, she had kissed them goodbye and they had not reciprocated.

566
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:12,000
They'd say, "No, no, we don't want to leave you."

567
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:16,000
At this point, Orpah says, "Yeah, you're right," and kisses her, which was the rational thing to do, right?

568
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:17,000
It made sense.

569
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:19,000
Orpah's like, "Yeah, I do want a husband.

570
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:20,000
Yeah, you are cursed.

571
00:35:20,000 --> 00:35:21,000
Yeah, this is going badly.

572
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:22,000
It's been nice knowing you.

573
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:23,000
I'm really going to miss you.

574
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:26,000
I'm going to go back to my mother's house and hope it gets better," right?

575
00:35:26,000 --> 00:35:28,000
"It's only going to get worse if we go to Israel."

576
00:35:28,000 --> 00:35:39,000
That was the reasonable thing to do if you're pursuing good things, if you're pursuing a material life, prosperity.

577
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:41,000
It's the only choice.

578
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:44,000
Ruth, apparently, was not pursuing prosperity.

579
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:51,000
So, instead of weeping and leaving, she clings to her and does the unreasonable thing.

580
00:35:52,000 --> 00:36:03,000
In verse 15, perhaps now frustrated because this crying girl is holding on to her instead of walking away like it was a reasonable thing to do, like she's been told to do multiple times, Naomi gets succinct and to the point.

581
00:36:03,000 --> 00:36:07,000
She says, "Your sister-in-law has gone back to her people and to her gods.

582
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:09,000
You are not like me.

583
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:10,000
Go back to your gods.

584
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:12,000
You know there's a difference between us."

585
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:16,000
And that seemed to be the point that set Ruth off, right?

586
00:36:16,000 --> 00:36:18,000
She didn't appreciate that.

587
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:24,000
This is the pivot point in the story of Ruth, is that moment when Naomi says, "Go back to your gods."

588
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:31,000
Ruth has this eloquent outburst that we still quote today, frequently out of context, but it's a beautiful statement, right?

589
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:34,000
Don't plead with me to turn my back.

590
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:36,000
Where you go, I'll go.

591
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:37,000
Where you sleep, I'll sleep.

592
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:39,000
Your people shall be my people.

593
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:40,000
Your God, my God.

594
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:42,000
Where you die, I'll die, and there will I be buried.

595
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:47,000
This says, "May the Lord do to me so and worse, if anything but death separates you from me."

596
00:36:47,000 --> 00:36:52,000
Most scholars think she made some sort of sign, "May the Lord do so to me, cut me off."

597
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:56,000
I tend to think that she'd been paying attention and listening to Naomi's subtext.

598
00:36:57,000 --> 00:37:06,000
Naomi has been quite justifiably complaining about all the stuff God's done to her because of her unfaithfulness when she left Bethlehem.

599
00:37:06,000 --> 00:37:14,000
And Ruth is saying, "If I'm unfaithful to you and leave like you did, may the Lord do to me everything you said he's doing to you and worse."

600
00:37:14,000 --> 00:37:16,000
If I leave you.

601
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:17,000
I'm not leaving you, old woman.

602
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:19,000
Right?

603
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:24,000
And I love the uncomfortable car ride.

604
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:27,000
When she saw that she was determined to go with her, she just stopped speaking to her about it.

605
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:28,000
Right?

606
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:29,000
She didn't agree.

607
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:31,000
She just said, "Ugh."

608
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:33,000
And they had this quiet walk the rest of the way of Bethlehem.

609
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:34,000
That's what it sounds like.

610
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:37,000
Because, Ruth's just not leaving.

611
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:39,000
It doesn't make sense.

612
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:41,000
Naomi's not ready to accept that.

613
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:44,000
But she desperately needs it.

614
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:46,000
Right?

615
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:55,000
This was a public declaration of a private determination to change the direction of her life and commit to it.

616
00:37:55,000 --> 00:38:00,000
She's leaving Chemosh and Moloch behind.

617
00:38:00,000 --> 00:38:05,000
So in this moment of decision, this is what sets up the rest of the book.

618
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:11,000
It's a moment of unnecessary kindness, but Ruth just won't leave her.

619
00:38:12,000 --> 00:38:22,000
And it not only changes the course of Naomi's life, but it changes the course of the family's life and of the city's life and the town of Bethlehem and the nation of Israel through her descendant David.

620
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:31,000
And it's led us to being here today through Christ, whose fulfillment of the prophecy of the kingdom of God growing out of the stump of David.

621
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:36,000
Because of the choice Ruth makes now and the choice Boaz is going to make in a few chapters.

622
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:41,000
But I don't think it changed Ruth.

623
00:38:41,000 --> 00:38:42,000
Right?

624
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:44,000
I think that change had already happened.

625
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:48,000
That's why she got frustrated when they said, "You're God."

626
00:38:48,000 --> 00:38:50,000
She's like, "It's not my God."

627
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:52,000
Right?

628
00:38:52,000 --> 00:38:55,000
She cements it in place when she's forced to declare an allegiance.

629
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:57,000
And she opts to push forward with simple,

630
00:38:57,000 --> 00:39:02,000
practical, faithful acts of righteousness day after day trying to help this old woman not starve.

631
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:06,000
She's looking out for the widowed and the dispossessed.

632
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:08,000
She's not trying to change history.

633
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:10,000
Sorry, the widowed and the dispossessed.

634
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:12,000
She's not trying to change history.

635
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:16,000
Even though she herself is now widowed and dispossessed by the effort.

636
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:17,000
Right?

637
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:22,000
She's just so committed to leaving the child sacrifice behind.

638
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:28,000
She's burning all her bridges and saying, "I'm going to repent.

639
00:39:28,000 --> 00:39:32,000
Whatever God does for us is what's going to happen."

640
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:35,000
And I think that's what it feels like to really repent.

641
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:38,000
We have the sinner's prayer, "Dear God, come into my life, I want to follow you."

642
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:41,000
Those are important words.

643
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:45,000
And what's behind it is an existing decision that you're leaving your old life behind.

644
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:46,000
Burning it.

645
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:48,000
You're dead to it.

646
00:39:48,000 --> 00:39:51,000
And you're moving a new direction.

647
00:39:51,000 --> 00:39:55,000
In the midst of our great suffering, what's required for life to spring out of ashes?

648
00:39:55,000 --> 00:39:57,000
Yielding our will to God.

649
00:39:57,000 --> 00:39:59,000
To be dead to our old life.

650
00:39:59,000 --> 00:40:01,000
Swearing allegiance to His righteousness.

651
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:04,000
And letting it inform our small daily actions like Ruth.

652
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:08,000
We cannot see, Ruth could not see the impact.

653
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:13,000
But God can use us, even in the midst of our suffering, to profoundly impact the world and the people around us.

654
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:14,000
And He will.

655
00:40:14,000 --> 00:40:16,000
Even though you probably won't see it.

656
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:24,000
So faithfulness is so much greater than the ashes of Ephratah, right?

657
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:27,000
Ephratah, life coming from the ashes.

658
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:30,000
When you die to self.

659
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:36,000
It's what Christ talks about in the passage we read this morning from John 12.

660
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:39,000
Unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone.

661
00:40:39,000 --> 00:40:46,000
But if it dies, Ruth said, I'm dead to my old life, right?

662
00:40:46,000 --> 00:40:50,000
It's so far, it's something I cannot achieve again.

663
00:40:50,000 --> 00:40:53,000
She hated her life.

664
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:55,000
She was no longer loyal to it.

665
00:40:55,000 --> 00:40:58,000
She loved the life of Christ, the life of God.

666
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:00,000
It dies, it bears much fruit.

667
00:41:00,000 --> 00:41:02,000
Whoever loves his life will lose it.

668
00:41:02,000 --> 00:41:04,000
Whoever hates his life in this world will keep it for eternal life.

669
00:41:04,000 --> 00:41:09,000
Hating your life and loving your life, those are words of loyalty.

670
00:41:09,000 --> 00:41:11,000
I'm going to cling to the new, Naomi.

671
00:41:11,000 --> 00:41:13,000
I'm going to hate the old.

672
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:15,000
I'm just going to burn the bridges and not even go back and see my mother again.

673
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:17,000
That's what Ruth did.

674
00:41:17,000 --> 00:41:20,000
This is not a lottery.

675
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:22,000
You're not throwing it out there to see if it works.

676
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:29,000
It's a determination, a desperate, solid hope that you're going to follow and burn the ships and the bridges behind you.

677
00:41:29,000 --> 00:41:37,000
Ruth became dead to her old life and she clung energetically, almost irrationally, to her new life with Naomi and Yahweh, which were the characters in our story.

678
00:41:38,000 --> 00:41:40,000
This is the model of repentance.

679
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:42,000
She burned her bridges.

680
00:41:42,000 --> 00:41:45,000
She energetically committed herself to this new life in whatever form it might take.

681
00:41:45,000 --> 00:41:52,000
She couldn't possibly have thought that it would be a prosperity.

682
00:41:53,000 --> 00:41:54,000
But she didn't toy around.

683
00:41:54,000 --> 00:41:55,000
She committed.

684
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:56,000
You have to not toy around.

685
00:41:56,000 --> 00:41:58,000
You have to turn from your old ways, pursue God.

686
00:41:58,000 --> 00:42:06,000
The God who has this book will go on to show us, will give you the strength and the means required to do so.

687
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:10,000
To pursue his righteousness far beyond what you can imagine.

688
00:42:10,000 --> 00:42:15,000
That's the lesson of suffering in Ruth.

689
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:18,000
I'm going to say a benediction here this morning.

690
00:42:19,000 --> 00:42:22,000
And then some people are going to come up.

691
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:34,000
And if you want to come and talk to somebody or pray with someone about repentance, about following God in a committed way, we'd love to talk to you, okay?

692
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:36,000
So I'm going to pray.

693
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:40,000
And then if you guys can, you know, quietly go out and get your kids, find good lunch.

694
00:42:40,000 --> 00:42:43,000
And those who want to stay and pray with us, we welcome it.

695
00:42:43,000 --> 00:42:46,000
Father God, thank you for the story of Ruth.

696
00:42:46,000 --> 00:42:51,000
Thank you for people like her in our history that make your righteousness available to us down to thousands of generations.

697
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:58,000
We pray that you would bless our efforts to follow you.

698
00:42:58,000 --> 00:43:01,000
Give us the strength when we have no strength.

699
00:43:01,000 --> 00:43:06,000
Let us leave behind the things that we just habitually follow like our own prosperity.

700
00:43:07,000 --> 00:43:11,000
Let us grow in our affection for you and our loyalty and our faithfulness to you, Lord.

701
00:43:11,000 --> 00:43:14,000
Give us people in our lives that will support that.

702
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:19,000
Let this church become a community that provides that support to others.

703
00:43:19,000 --> 00:43:21,000
Thank you in Jesus' name.

704
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:24,000
You guys go with God and have a great week.

705
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:26,000
We're going to be here.

706
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:27,000
We're going to be here.

707
00:43:27,000 --> 00:43:28,000
We're going to be here.
